4️⃣ Palindrome Partitioning III (Hard)
🔹 Problem Statement

Given a string s and an integer k, partition the string into k non-empty substrings such that:

The sum of the number of character changes required to make each substring a palindrome is minimized.

Return the minimum number of changes required.

🔹 Example 1
Input:
s = "abc"
k = 2

Output: 1
Explanation:

Possible partitions:

a | bc → (0 + 1 change) = 1
ab | c → (1 + 0 change) = 1

Minimum = 1

🔹 Example 2
Input:
s = "aabbc"
k = 3

Output: 0

Partition:

aa | bb | c

Already palindromes → 0 changes

🔎 Intuition

This is a DP Partitioning Problem

Two key ideas:

1️⃣ Precompute cost to make any substring palindrome
2️⃣ Use DP to partition into k groups

💡 Step 1: Precompute Palindrome Cost

For substring s[i…j]

Cost = number of mismatched pairs.

Example:

"abc"
a != c → 1 change

So cost = 1.

We build a 2D matrix:

cost[i][j]
💡 Step 2: DP Definition

Let:

dp[i][k] = minimum changes needed for substring starting at i with k partitions left

Transition:

dp[i][k] = min over j from i to n-1 of:

cost[i][j] + dp[j+1][k-1]

Base cases:

If k == 1 → cost[i][n-1]
If remaining characters < k → infinity
⚙️ Algorithm

1️⃣ Precompute cost[][]
2️⃣ Use DP with memoization
3️⃣ Try all partition endings

💻 Java Code
class Solution {
    
    private int[][] cost;
    private Integer[][] memo;
    private String s;
    private int n;
    
    public int palindromePartition(String s, int k) {
        this.s = s;
        this.n = s.length();
        
        // Step 1: Precompute palindrome cost
        cost = new int[n][n];
        
        for (int length = 2; length <= n; length++) {
            for (int i = 0; i + length - 1 < n; i++) {
                int j = i + length - 1;
                
                cost[i][j] = cost[i+1][j-1];
                if (s.charAt(i) != s.charAt(j)) {
                    cost[i][j]++;
                }
            }
        }
        
        // Step 2: Memoization
        memo = new Integer[n][k+1];
        
        return dp(0, k);
    }
    
    private int dp(int start, int k) {
        if (k == 1) {
            return cost[start][n-1];
        }
        
        if (memo[start][k] != null) {
            return memo[start][k];
        }
        
        int min = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        
        for (int end = start; end <= n - k; end++) {
            min = Math.min(min,
                    cost[start][end] + dp(end + 1, k - 1));
        }
        
        memo[start][k] = min;
        return min;
    }
}
🧠 Code Explanation (Step-by-Step)
✅ Precompute Palindrome Cost

For each substring:

cost[i][j] =
    cost[i+1][j-1] + (s[i] != s[j] ? 1 : 0)

This avoids recalculating palindrome checks repeatedly.

✅ DP Recursion

We try every possible split:

Example:

s = "abc"
k = 2

Try:

a | bc
ab | c

Pick minimum.

